"Shows formal reporting relationships and organizational units within a clearly defined institution or team.\n\nUse when: Explain reporting lines and management scope. Orient new colleagues to units and their formal leadership.\n\nAvoid when: Informal collaboration or task-specific accountability is the actual question.\n\nVisual grammar: Boxes identify positions or organizational units at a consistent level. Hierarchy connectors denote formal reporting relationships. Separate line styles distinguish approved secondary relationships when needed.\n\nBuild: Choose whether boxes represent positions, people or units. Start from the relevant authority and place direct reports below it. Identify vacancies, shared appointments and special reporting arrangements explicitly.\n\nCheck: Every relationship matches the current approved structure. Peer boxes represent comparable organizational entities. Secondary reporting does not obscure the primary reporting path.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming the chart describes every collaboration or decision right. Using a person's name without clarifying the position they occupy.\n\nExample: A museum chart places collections, visitor services and operations under the director, with vacant positions explicitly marked.\n\nAlternatives: Organizational network map, RACI matrix."
